Sacred affords a claim on how something is treated. It can ask you to slow down, keep your hands off, remember, or make room. Sometimes it gives something the right to stay even after it has lost its use.

A title works in both directions. The sacred thing gets protection. Whoever gets to declare it sacred gains some authority over what others may do with it. That’s why it matters who bestows the title and what the title protects.

Take the second mug. To anyone else, it’s a mug taking up space. To the person who still reaches for it, its place in the cupboard is a way of keeping someone in the house. What the sacred affords, there, is permission to leave a place for someone who was here.